Comparison (Retest-Based)\"\n13. \"MIA/Min-K++ on SFT Contamination\"\n14. \"ARC-C Benchmark Validation (Small Benchmark)\"\n15. \"HT vs lz* Post-RL Differentiation\"\n16. \"Mistral n=1 Supporting Evidence\"\n17. \"Full Baseline Comparison Table (Frontier Ranking)\"\n18. \"Gemma-4-31B Controlled Contamination (Conditional)\"\n\nNow I'm mapping each experiment to its corresponding section and table in the paper. The Qwen pilot goes to section 4.2 with Table 1, the full four-family contamination analysis spans Tables 2 and 3 in the same section, and I'm working through the rest of the experiments to place them in their appropriate locations throughout sections 5, 6, and beyond. Now I'm mapping out the actual paper structure section by section, allocating space and experiments to each part. The introduction and background will be brief, then I'll dedicate the bulk of the paper to the controlled contamination experiments, baseline comparisons, and a detailed analysis section that breaks down robustness findings, ablations, statistical power, domain-level effects, IRT recovery, and natural experiments.\n\nI'm realizing the page budget is tight—roughly 16 columns across 8 pages in a two-column format—so I need to keep the discussion and conclusion minimal and integrate limitations throughout rather than as a separate section. Let me just start writing the LaTeX and focus on getting the content down cleanly without overthinking the exact layout.\n\nWriting LaTeX document... Now I'm pulling together the specific experimental results—contamination effects at different thresholds, the accuracy and memorization fractions, theta shift patterns, and the cross-family detection metrics across the model families. Now I'm looking at the cross-benchmark results at 15% degradation—MMLU shows a power coefficient of 0.979 with strong memorization at 0.35, while HellaSwag and GSM8K have weaker power values around 0.75 and 0.59. The leaderboard audit experiment was abandoned due to discrimination failures, but the natural experiment with 21 model pairs shows significant effects on HellaSwag and GSM8K with p-values under 0.01, though MMLU's results are still pending.\n\nMoving into the refit experiments, I'm comparing 3PL and 2PL models across different sample sizes—the expanded 3PL model with 178 items shows stronger discrimination (a_r=0.127) compared to the 2PL version. Then in the 4PL versus 3PL comparison at 25% contamination, the 4PL model dramatically outperforms on MMLU with a power gain of 44.7 percentage points, while HellaSwag shows a smaller gap between the two approaches.\n\nAcross the benchmark suite—GSM8K, ARC-C, and HumanEval—the 4PL model consistently achieves higher power gains, though the false alarm rates reveal a critical trade-off: 4PL maintains a 5% FAR while 3PL ranges from 4.7% to 16.5%, and 2PL balloons to 50-100%. Finally, when comparing against baseline models like lz* and HT, the 4PL approach with binary parameterization produces the strongest discrimination index (d=-3.42), substantially outperforming simpler models like Guttman and U3. Looking at the post-RL results, the memorization metrics show significant degradation across the board—lz* drops from -3.42 to -1.5 with only 44% retention, and Min-K++ essentially becomes random after training. The memo_frac plummets from 0.38 to 0.12, indicating the model is forgetting memorized content, though accuracy still reaches 0.74.\n\nNow examining performance across broader benchmarks: ARC-C and GSM8K show strong alignment between theory and simulation, while MATH, BBH, and MMLU reveal larger performance gaps with d-values ranging from -1.2 to -3.4, suggesting the model struggles more on complex reasoning tasks.\n\nLooking at domain-level performance across different models, Gemini-1.5 maintains relatively uniform gains across STEM, humanities, social sciences, and other domains, whereas Yi-Lightning shows extreme uniform improvements across all areas, while Doubao-Pro exhibits selective negative performance in humanities and social sciences despite gains elsewhere.\n\nConStat validation achieved an AUROC of 0.78 with computational overhead of 3 retest runs and 8 GPU hours, and these results have already been incorporated into the earlier experiments.\n\nNow examining the Min-K++ and MIA-loss approaches across different training stages—base, SFT, and post-RL—along with their perplexity metrics, then moving into ARC-C contamination analysis showing how performance degrades with increasing contamination levels and the corresponding delta values, and finally looking at how the lz* metric behaves in the post-RL setting. Now I have all the experimental results compiled. I'm organizing the paper into a logical structure that groups the controlled contamination experiments, baseline comparisons, and additional analyses into coherent sections with appropriate subsections for each experiment or method.\n\nWriting LaTeX document...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":7528,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20644,"cache_read_input_tokens":23635,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20644},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ea1e9cd0-76ef-4277-8837-49ffa06069a9","uuid":"31b766fd-dda7-421f-9f91-7ed14def8d86"}
